0","md5":"cec0cfd94c23854788697e25580dce63","crc32":"33cb53d3","sha1":"567992a6f257ba906e6182a591de353a895869b3","format":"Archive BitTorrent"},{"name":"gunsmoke_ranch_files.xml","source":"metadata","format":"XML Metadata","md5":"016f7e2529e0f5f31c704f7482133cab","summation":"md5"},{"name":"gunsmoke_ranch_meta.xml","source":"original","format":"Metadata","mtime":"1762242600","size":"2089","md5":"f0ff90f2fb2f503162fcaaadcdbdf77c","crc32":"4dc06843","sha1":"f48250ac5d18d5388664a603a1b83f97b8d41253"},{"name":"gunsmoke_ranch_reviews.xml","source":"original","format":"Metadata","mtime":"1720037632","size":"7380","md5":"70a8e1340213353d92b83d66e18e9a56","crc32":"87dc130c","sha1":"f1f14d31a05781d5ac9470ab4f50b1915f987d91"}],"files_count":67,"item_last_updated":1762242601,"item_size":4274056697,"metadata":{"mediatype":"movies","identifier":"gunsmoke_ranch","publicdate":"2004-02-09 11:00:33","creator":"Sol C. Siegel","publisher":"Republic Pictures","description":"The Three Mesquiteers protect settlers against an evil politician trying to steal their land after a flood.\n\nYou can find more information regarding this film on <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0028971/\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">its IMDb page</a>.","date":"1937","licenseurl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/publicdomain/","color":"b&w","sound":"sound","collection":["feature_films_unsorted","feature_films","moviesandfilms"],"title":"Gunsmoke Ranch","uploader":"skipe@mindspring.com","addeddate":"2004-02-09 11:00:33","adder":"AV Geek Skip","credits":"Robert Livingston - Stony Brooke\nRay Corrigan - Tucson Smith\nMax Terhune - Lullaby Joslin\nKenneth Harlan - Flagg\nJean Carmen - Marion\nSammy McKim - Jimmy\nOscar and Elmer - Themselves\nBurr Caruth - Warren\nAllen Connor - Reggie\nYakima Canutt - Spider\nHorace B. Carpenter - Larkin\nJane Keckley - Mathilda\nRobert Walker - Williams\nJack Ingram - Jed\nLoren Riebe - Hank\nJack Kirk - Sheriff\nVinegar Roan - Zeke\nWes Warner - Old Man\nJack Padjan - Duke\n\nDirector: Joseph Kane\nWriter: Oliver Drake\nProducer: Sol C. Siegel\nCinematography: Gus Peterson\nEditing: Russell Schoengarth ","director":"Joseph Kane","pick":"0","runtime":"52:47:00","updatedate":"2005-01-13 09:36:44","updater":"simon c","subject":"western","numeric_id":"4950","type":"MovingImage","proddate":"1937","collectionid":"gunsmoke_ranch","backup_location":"ia903600_5","closed_captioning":"no","collection_added":["feature_films","moviesandfilms"]},"reviews":[{"reviewbody":"Pretty funny-bad Western here, which feature that all-time famous (sarcasm) trio, The Three Mesquiteers, as they help people who have just escaped a flood (which caused DESOLATION, PESTILATION and FAMINE, (so say the cue cards). They are offered ranch land in Arizona by a unscrupulous shyster, and it's up to the three to make sure Justice Is Served! So in less in an hour, we have laughably bad fights, a joke of a song, scary stunt handling, a guy doing a non-stop comedy routine with a dummy, and rather offensive jibes at stuttering people. All this, and this rather curious confusion with the 1930's wardrobe mixed in with western drag. Highly strange, but not really worth your time.","reviewtitle":"I feel shystered myself!","stars":"2","reviewer":"Spuzz","createdate":"2006-01-12 14:38:44","reviewdate":"2006-01-12 14:38:44"},{"reviewbody":"This may not be the best of the 51 Three Mesquiteers movies Republic made from 1936-43, but it evokes nostalsia for those of us who saw them when they were a stable of early 1950s TV (or those few survivors who recall seeing them in theatres).\nAlthough Ray \"Crash\" Corrigan, Bob Livingston and Max Terune (the ventriloquist who seems somewhat out of place), who star in this one are the actors most familiar in the roles of William Colt McDonald's characters, others, including John Wayne, played during the series' long run. \nThe actual time of the Mesquiteers films shifted from movie to movie, with some set in (then) contemporary times and others set in the old west (and different periods at that).\nThese westerns have plenty of \"B\" action in which you occasionally can tell a roundhouse punch misses its mark, although the recipient falls anyway and counting bullets fired suggests some secret ammo feed.\nThey're nonetheless my favorites among all of the \"B\" westerns, always leavened by humor, some of which works and some of which is funny because it doesn't. --Allan Maurer","reviewtitle":"The Three Mesquiteers Ride Again","stars":"3","reviewer":"Allandale","createdate":"2006-12-26 14:16:15","reviewdate":"2006-12-26 14:16:15"},{"reviewbody":"It may be worth mentioning that these characters were the basis of the contemporary film - \"The three Amigos.\" I had no idea there were 51 films in the series though. Worth a post modern deconstuctive analysis or two (heh) as transcendent magical realism","reviewtitle":"No peyote here","reviewer":"Tamlin","reviewdate":"2007-05-10 07:52:25","createdate":"2007-05-10 07:52:25","stars":"4"},{"reviewbody":"I think that perhaps only old fellows like myself can really enjoy these old cowboy movies because of the nostalgia it produces in our old brains. As children this genre was what we fed on. It must be remembered that in those days we didn't have TV and we also didn't have any money. We could go to one of these films for just 5 cents. Children today can't possibly know what grand times we had back then watching these corny old films.","reviewtitle":"Old-time cowboy movies.","reviewer":"Budro","reviewdate":"2009-07-07 18:06:27","createdate":"2009-07-07 18:06:27","stars":"4"},{"reviewbody":"Ray \"Crash\" Corrigan was a very popular B movie actor during the 1930s and 1940s.  The nickname Crash was thought up by studio execs because it sounded similar to \"Flash\" of the Flash Gordon series.  Corrigan played in a similar series for Republic Pictures called \"Undersea Kingdom\".  His character in that series was \"Crash Corrigan\".  \n\nIn 1937, Corrigan bought over 200 acres in Simi Valley where he built a movie ranch.  Many westerns were filmed there.  In the 1950s and 1960s, TV shows such as Have Gun Will Travel, Rin Tin Tin, The Lone Ranger and even episodes of Star Trek were shot at Corriganville.\n\nToday the original buildings are all gone and Corriganville is a regional park.","reviewtitle":"\"Crash\" Corrigan Rides Again!","reviewer":"Big Boomer","reviewdate":"2017-04-29 02:03:10","createdate":"2017-04-29 02:03:10","stars":"5"},{"reviewbody":"Another action packed movie starring \"The Three Mesquiteers\": vocalist Robert Livingstone, his constant rival for the women they meet Ray Corrigan, and ventriloquist Max Terhune and his dummy Elmer. Here they foil particularly nasty land swindle plans of Kenneth Harlan (Phineas T. Flagg) and his henchmen who include regular western bad guys Allen Connor (Reggie Allen), Yakima Canutt (Spider) and others. Julia Thayer (aka Jean Carmen) is the attractive love interest that Livingstone and Corrigan, as usual, fight over. She could actually ride a horse well and started off in film by being a double in horse riding scenes. She was in 21 films - 20 between 1933-39 and one in 1953, and is credited in 12 (incl 2 Three Stooges shorts). There is a hard to take comedy interlude involving a speech by someone who stutters and a few offensive comments out of Terhune's dummy. Livingstone sings a nice song with an uncredted western band. Terhune brings his dummy Elmer to life here and there and does some animal imitations, both for short spots so as not to get tiresome. And, yes there is enough action to keep the movie exciting - fighting, gun play, ambushes and riding. And they ride off into the prairie. And, its Pestilence, not Pestilation as noted by 1st reviewer Spuzz.","reviewtitle":"The 3 Mesquiteer Right Wrongs As Usual","reviewer":"Poohbah70","reviewer_itemname":"@poohbah70","reviewdate":"2019-12-10 04:01:06","createdate":"2019-11-01 01:12:14","stars":"3"},{"reviewbody":"Watching the older actors appearing in this 1937 film, it struck me that the folks born of that 1870-1880\u2019s time were a jollier bunch than folks today.","reviewtitle":"19th Century People","reviewer":"qwerty1235","reviewer_itemname":"@qwerty1235","reviewdate":"2019-12-01 00:12:32","createdate":"2019-12-01 00:12:32","stars":"0"},{"reviewtitle":"First TV Showing?","reviewbody":"It may have been the first time the movie was listed individually, but using the Newspapers.com advanced search engine, the earliest TV airing I was able to track down was Saturday November 25, 1961 at 4pm CST on KSD-TV 5, St Louis, MO.  The time slot was for The Three Mesquiteers film series, edited where necessary to fit a 50-minute run time. ","stars":"3","reviewer":"Braveologist65","reviewdate":"2024-07-01 18:16:53","createdate":"2024-07-01 18:16:53","reviewer_itemname":"@braveologist65"}],"server":"dn801204.us.archive.org","simplelists":{"solo_manifest":{"solo_manifest_dn801204":{"sys_changed_by":{"source":"task","task_id":"5451769733"},"sys_last_changed":"2026-06-19 16:22:02.137084"}}},"solo":true,"uniq":909583252,"workable_servers":["dn801204.us.archive.org"]}